          forms: ["[Pl] + {反面|はんめん}／{半面|はんめん}\n［[なA~~だ~~]な　~~[N]だ~~］"],
          formNotes: [
            { ja: "「[なA]／[N] である + {反面|はんめん}」の形もある。", en: "The form なA／N + である + 反面 is also used.", gen: true },
          ],
          examples: [
            { ja: "来日前は留学に{期待|きたい}する{反面|はんめん}、不安も大きかった。", en: "Before coming to Japan, I was looking forward to studying abroad, but at the same time I was also very anxious." },
            { ja: "IT{機器|きき}は{多機能化|たきのうか}が進んで、便利な{反面|はんめん}、{操作|そうさ}が{複雑|ふくざつ}すぎて使いこなせない人が増えている。", en: "IT devices have become more and more multifunctional; while this is convenient, a growing number of people can't make full use of them because operating them is too complicated." },
            { ja: "{高層|こうそう}マンションは{設備|せつび}がよくて{快適|かいてき}に生活できる{反面|はんめん}、一度{停電|ていでん}すると{設備|せつび}がまったく使えなくなるという問題もある。", en: "High-rise apartments have good facilities and let you live comfortably, but there's also the problem that once the power goes out, the facilities become completely unusable." },
            { ja: "{木製|もくせい}や{紙製|かみせい}の{植木鉢|うえきばち}は{通気性|つうきせい}に{優|すぐ}れている{反面|はんめん}、{乾燥|かんそう}しやすいという{欠点|けってん}もある。", en: "Wooden and paper flowerpots are excellent in terms of breathability, but they also have the drawback of drying out easily." },
            { ja: "国民の{長寿|ちょうじゅ}は{喜|よろこ}ばしい{反面|はんめん}、国の{財政負担|ざいせいふたん}が増えるという問題もある。", en: "The longevity of the population is something to be happy about, but it also brings the problem of an increased financial burden on the state." },
          ],
          deepDive: "**反面** literally means *the reverse side*; **半面** *one half, one side*. Both are read はんめん and used the same way: **A反面、B** = *while A, on the other hand B*, where A and B are **two opposite aspects of the same thing** (the book: 2つの反対の面や視点). Typically one is a merit and the other a drawback: IT機器は多機能化が進んで、便利な反面、操作が複雑すぎて使いこなせない人が増えている (*IT devices have become multifunctional; they're convenient, but more and more people can't make full use of them because they're too complicated*).\n\nConnection (the book): plain form + 反面; な-adjectives take な (便利な反面); a noun can't take だ here, so use **である** (the ＊ note): 学生である反面. ✗学生の反面, ✗学生だ反面. **その反面** can begin a new sentence, like その一方で.\n\nIt also describes **mixed feelings** in one person: 来日前は留学に期待する反面、不安も大きかった (*before coming to Japan I was looking forward to studying abroad, but I was also very anxious*).\n\nDon't confuse it with **〜一方（で）** (#61):\n- 反面 needs the **same subject** with two faces. ✗兄は背が高い反面、弟は低い (two people) → ✓兄は背が高い一方、弟は低い (*my older brother is tall, while the younger one is short*).\n- 一方 can contrast different things and also pair two parallel activities (料理を発表する一方、研究もしている, *she presents dishes and also does research*). 反面 always implies **opposition**, so it can't join two neutral activities.\n\nOther relatives:\n- **〜ものの** (#39), *although ~*: concedes a fact, then says the expected result didn't follow: 買ったものの、まだ使っていない (*I bought it, but I haven't used it yet*). 反面 weighs two sides; ものの reports a letdown.\n- **〜ながら（も）** (#15), *although*: also concessive, not a weighing of pros and cons: 狭いながらも快適だ (*small, but comfortable*).\n- **〜が / けれども**: general contrast.\n\nStyle: written and analytical; ideal for essays that weigh pros and cons, as in the sample: 成果が期待される反面、家畜の被害のおそれもある (*results are expected, but there's also a risk of harm to livestock*).\n\nJLPT tip: 反面 is usually followed by a disadvantage, often ending 〜という問題もある / 〜という欠点もある (examples ③–⑤), so the advantage comes first. The Check: アクリルはガラスと比べて軽く衝撃に強い反面、表面に傷がついて透明度が下がりやすい (*acrylic is lighter and more impact-resistant than glass, but its surface scratches and loses transparency easily*).",
